    "reviewer": "jonf",
    "reviewed_at": "2026-08-01",
    "confidence": "high",
    "summary": "Sao Paulo was relatively easy to assess overall; the rubric fit the NBR 9050 infrastructure and reviewer-rated confidence is HIGH. Two things made it difficult: mid-block captures where curb ramp assessment meant looking back at the closest intersection, often too far away to see clearly; and a set of intersections with white paint along the curb that rarely have actual curb ramps even where crosswalks exist - a pattern that visibly confused the model, and initially the reviewer, until the pattern was recognized.",
    "caveats": [
      "Mid-block captures. More than 10 panos had the capture camera in the middle of a street segment, so assessment required looking back at the closest intersection - too far away to see things clearly. This is the main driver of the abstentions, which are the benchmark highest: 14.7% of detections and 32.1% of missed marks are unsure (budapest was 8.5%). Given HIGH reviewer confidence these read as honest distance-driven abstentions, not rubric failure.",
      "White-painted curbs. A set of Sao Paulo intersections carry white paint along the curb but rarely have actual curb ramps, even where crosswalks are present. The model fired on these repeatedly (reviewer impression, not a measurement), and the reviewer was initially confused by them too before reconciling on the pattern - a candidate split-specific false-positive mechanism worth checking in the FP taxonomy."
    ]
  },
